<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Transkei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1976">1976</a>–<a href="/years/1994">1994</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Transkei was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustan</a>, or homeland, for Xhosa-speaking people in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, with its capital at Umtata (Mthatha). It received self-government in 1963 and in 1976 became the first homeland declared independent under apartheid, a status no other country recognised. It was reincorporated into South Africa in 1994.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/transkei.jpg" alt="Transkei" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Makaristos · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Transkei.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The South African government set up the area as one of two homelands for Xhosa-speaking people in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-province">Cape Province</a>, the other being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ciskei">Ciskei</a>, and Prime Minister <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hendrik-verwoerd">Hendrik Verwoerd</a> gave it nominal autonomy in 1963. The first election was won by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-party-uganda">Democratic Party</a>, whose founder, Chief Victor Poto, opposed independence for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantustan">Bantustans</a>, but the government was formed by the Transkei National Independence Party. Of the 109 members of the regional parliament, 45 were elected and 64 were chiefs holding seats ex officio. The name means the area beyond the Kei river.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Transkei became nominally independent in 1976 under Chief Kaiser Daliwonga Matanzima, prime minister until 1979 and then president until 1986. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-vorster">B. J. Vorster</a>, defended the step by invoking the right of every people to control its own affairs. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations-general-assembly">United Nations General Assembly</a> declared the independence invalid, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> condemned it as designed to consolidate apartheid. The economy stayed dependent on South Africa, where much of the population was recruited to work in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/witwatersrand">the Rand</a> mines.</p><p>In April 1978, over a territorial dispute about East Griqualand, Matanzima announced a break in diplomatic ties with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, but backed down because of Transkei's dependence on economic aid. In 1984 a student protest at the University of Transkei was stormed by riot police and a student, Patricia Cele, died; a state of emergency followed that lasted over three years. Matanzima retired on 20 February 1986 and his brother George succeeded him. In 1987 General Bantu Holomisa forced George Matanzima out and then overthrew Stella Sigcau in a coup. Transkei was reincorporated into South Africa on 27 April 1994 and became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-cape">Eastern Cape</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Transkei had three disconnected sections covering 45,000 square kilometres, the main one lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mtamvuna-river">Umtamvuna</a> and Great Kei rivers, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drakensberg">Drakensberg</a> on its eastern and western sides. Xhosa was the sole official language by its constitution, although Sotho and English were used for laws. An estimate of 1982 gave the population as about 2.3 million, with roughly 400,000 citizens living permanently outside its borders. The Transkei Defence Force, formed in October 1976 with about 2,000 men, was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>'s forces in 1994.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Transkei” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Grand apartheid assigned Africans to ten ethnic ‘homelands’, four of which, <b>Transkei</b> (1976), Bophuthatswana (1977), Venda (1979) and Ciskei (1981), were declared independent, although no other country recognised them.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-south-africa">Republic of South Africa · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
